January six committee tomorrow in more detail. But&nbsp;
Trump attacks the committee members as being bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:05.460
people. He says there was no evidence he is just&nbsp;
a wacky, wacky, wacky guy. Now let's talk about an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:13.560
insanity defense. Number one, it's important to&nbsp;
remember that an insanity plea is not a get out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:20.760
of jail free card. It's a defense that wants to&nbsp;
mitigate the punishment for a crime by arguing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:26.040
defendant was not in control of their actions due&nbsp;
to mental illness. The defendant didn't understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:31.440
the consequences of their decisions due to mental&nbsp;
illness, which means that even if you success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.020
succeed at defending someone on the basis of&nbsp;
insanity, you can still be found guilty and face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.460
punishment, like being confined to a mental health&nbsp;
facility, for example. Now, I don't actually think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:45.600
any of that's going to happen with Trump tomorrow.&nbsp;
We'll have Jamie Raskin's recent comments, what he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.960
believes is going to happen to Trump that. But&nbsp;
that's a difficulty or something to understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:55.740
I would say, about an insanity defense. One of&nbsp;
the biggest challenges in pleading insanity is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:01.740
burden of proof, because in order to successfully&nbsp;
argue the defense of insanity, the defendant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:07.500
must present evidence that they were suffering&nbsp;
from a mental illness at the time of the crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.780
that that caused them to lack the capacity to&nbsp;
understand that their actions were wrong or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:19.020
illegal or whatever the case may be. That is often&nbsp;
very difficult to do because the defendant needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.240
to provide detailed information about their mental&nbsp;
state and the symptoms they were experiencing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:30.780
They bring in medical experts. And it is a&nbsp;
it's difficult to meet that burden of proof,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:36.000
particularly if Donald Trump is otherwise,&nbsp;
quote, functioning, although we would argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.840
he wasn't really functioning. It wasn't really&nbsp;
working, but functioning as president of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:45.600
United States. There's also the stigma that&nbsp;
surrounds mental illness because there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.800
many people who view mental illness. This is&nbsp;
a weakness or a character flaw rather than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:56.640
medical condition. So even in a case where the&nbsp;
defense actually can meet that burden of proof.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.360
There are still jurors as a general&nbsp;
that people who may end up on the jury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:11.220
which might not be easily convinced that what&nbsp;
they were seeing were the out of control actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:17.700
of someone suffering from mental illness rather&nbsp;
than just somebody who broke the law. So insanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:23.820
defense is an uphill battle. If it's Trump, I&nbsp;
think it's a particularly uphill battle because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:30.780
the other circumstances I mentioned. In addition&nbsp;
